hat might occur in a  patient's infection  another important point about detecting  this isolate  as a carbopylase producer is that kpc is  a transmissible carbopytamize and  therefore the isolate could have been a  source of kpc transmission  the bottom line with molecular tests of  positive blood cultures is that they are  definitely worth doing as positive  results can save lives  this slide reinforces the point that  negative molecular tests can be falsely  negative which is important to remember  the table data  from a study in which the separate and  verigene assays were used to test  250 well-characterized cavapinomas  producers  the centered pca targets  are the carbopenemias family is referred  to as the big five  ndm  kpc  imp  vim and oxa48  the verigene micro array  has more targets but requires an  organism identification to detect them  the limited ranges of the two tests  meant that met both were incapable  of detecting many carbopanamas and coded  in genes  carbopenometers shown in blue are not  targets for these tests  therefore both tests missed genes  encoding sme me nmca gim and spm  carbopenomases  in addition cepheid cannot  detect genes for the  oxocarbopenemosis of a senator back  berman again  among us target genes  cepheid had problems with the emf family  it could not detect genes encoding imp-7  imp-8  imp-13 in 14  imp 18 and imp 27.  to me this problem indicates that its  target range is not the big five  but more likely should be called the big  4.5  verigene did not detect kpc or ndm genes  of morganella seratia  klebsiella variacola  hafnia providencia and salmonella  because it could not identify these  organisms  these findings are just for carbopylase  gene detection molecular tests can also  miss genes for espl's and mcs  so summing up all this molecular tests  are helpful if you get a positive result  unfortunately  their target ranges are not keeping up  with what's happening with antimicrobial  resistance  they are not stand-alone tests  before moving on to other tests  performed on positive blood cultures  what about bypassing blood cultures  altogether and using molecular tests  directly on whole blood  doing molecular tests directly on whole  blood would eliminate the delay of  waiting for a blood culture to go  positive  this is theoretically feasible with  current multiplex pcr tests for target  pathogens and resistance genes  advantages could be that unlike blood  cultures antibiotics don't interfere  with these tests  and they have the potential to be  helpful if blood cultures are negative  but  unfortunately  these tests are less sensitive and  current multiplex pcr tests on positive  blood cultures  and this makes them unreliable  t2 magnetic resonance  is more sensitive than multiplex  pcr this is pathogen-specific pcr  with miniaturized magnetic resonance  after pca after the pcr amplification  step the amplified dna is detected by  hybridization to probe enriched  superparamagnetic nanoparticles  which causes large changes in the t2  signal  and confers superior sensitivity  this technology offers great potential  but currently there is the disadvantage  of limited target ranges  t2 contests for only  five species of candida and not  including the very important spreading  treatment resistant candida aurus  similarly it can only test for five  bacterial species  and this approach does not include  antimicrobial resistance targets  the omics technologies have great  potential for the future  in-depth studies  of the classes of biochemicals listed in  this slide  will eventually help us to understand  the interplay between host and pathogen  and improve sexist sepsis  recognition  and appropriateness of antimicrobial  therapy on an individual patient basis  at this time there remain many technical  challenges to resolve  as one example in transcriptomics  the analysis of rna transcripts  dynamic changes in transcription occur  that result in a potential lack of  reproducibility and lack of  understanding of the results  in certain patient populations such as  those with neutropenia or solid organ  transplants  the sensitivity and specificity  of these types of technologies  have not been sufficiently studied to  use them as stand-alone tests  and they require great investment in  specialized equipment and high levels of  technical expertise to analyze and  interpret data  they also require standardization of  methods and bioinformatics tools  finally  going back to testing positive blood  cultures  let's look at some growth-based  technologies but not the slow  conventional tests that were in use 50  years ago and are still being used today  this slide shows two rapid technologies  that can be performed directly on  positive blood cultures  the accelerate pheno  and the ucast rest method  ras is the abbreviation for rapid  antimicrobial susceptibility tests  briefly  the pheno is an identification  antibiotic susceptibility instrument  identifications can be done by fish  technology in approximately two hours  or with a laboratory's own system  to me the susceptibility test is what  will determine the future of athena  is based on novel technology  in which cell growth is monitored  by powerful imaging technology that  compares the morphology of bacterial  cells  in the presence and absence of  antimicrobials  mics are obtained in approximately seven  hours  this is a neat concept  and the phenol is now in a number of  clinical laboratories  some laboratories use it as a reliable  standalone test  and report results without reservation  other laboratories do not have the same  comfort level  and back up phenotests  being a result with conventional tests  sometimes finding disagreement between  the pheno and conv and the conventional  results  this raises the issue  of whether early antibiotic  susceptibility results are okay to  report for serious infections  if there is uncertainty about their  accuracy  no susceptibility test is perfec